# WORKAROUND(Windows native-build path length, Phase 1 Plan 5 native spike):
# pnpm's default "isolated" node-linker places every package under
# node_modules/.pnpm/<name>@<version>_<peer-dep-hash>/node_modules/<name>/...
# — for packages with several peer deps (react-native-nitro-modules,
# @op-engineering/op-sqlite, react-native-worklets) that hashed folder name
# alone is 120-190+ characters. Combined with Android's own CMake/NDK build
# path (android/.cxx/Debug/<hash>/x86_64/CMakeFiles/<target>.dir/<source>.o),
# the full object-file path exceeds Windows' CMAKE_OBJECT_PATH_MAX (250 chars)
# and MAX_PATH (260 chars), causing native compilation to fail with
# "ninja: error: manifest 'build.ninja' still dirty after 100 tries" — object
# files silently fail to write because their path is too long.
# `node-linker=hoisted` resolves every package to node_modules/<name> directly
# (no hashed .pnpm segment), which is short enough to stay under both limits.
# Remove this once either (a) Windows long-path support is enabled at the OS
# level for every dev machine building Android locally, or (b) pnpm supports
# a shorter virtual-store hashing scheme.
node-linker=hoisted
